scope: |
  MEASURED 2026-07-28, and it is the half `ci-runs-cancelled-under-churn` does not cover.
  That bug added a `concurrency` group, which collapses the queue to "one running + one pending".
  Correct, but it does not decide WHICH commit gets the pending slot — so a docs commit pushed
  seconds after a code commit EVICTS the code commit's pending run, and the code commit never gets
  a verdict at all.

  The numbers, over the last hour on `main`:
    - 80 commits, 58 of them without `[skip ci]` (i.e. they create a CI run)
    - **43 of those 58 change ONLY `.md` or `.work/` files** — bookkeeping, no code
    - `sbt — compile and test` takes 3h16m (measured on the last run that finished: 17:55:20→21:11:40)
    - 62 CI runs queued right now; LAST `success` on ci.yml was 2026-07-27T16:31:33Z
  So ~74% of the CI load carries no code, and it is starving the runs that do.

  Fix: `paths-ignore` on the push/pull_request triggers for `**.md` and `.work/**`. A commit that
  touches code AND a doc still runs (paths-ignore only skips when EVERY changed path matches), so
  this cannot hide a code change. It is enforcement rather than discipline: `[skip ci]` already
  exists and 22 of 80 commits used it, but the other 58 did not — a rule that depends on every
  agent remembering it is not a rule.

  NOT changing: the concurrency block, the job structure, or `timeout-minutes`. Those are the other
  claim's territory and its reasoning is sound; this is strictly about not spending the one pending
  slot on commits that cannot affect the build.
next: plan into SPRINT (blocked while claim-mutex holds it — record here meanwhile), implement, verify the trigger with a docs-only and a code+docs commit
